00:01:02.285 --> 00:01:03.118
- Good.

00:01:03.118 --> 00:01:03.990
- So we know you're a busy guy

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:04.950
and we only have a few minutes

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:06.200
so we're just going to hop right into it,

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:08.370
if that's all good with you.

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:10.930
So Joey and I, he's 17, I'm 19.

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:14.110
We started our first
media startup last year

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:15.590
and we launched our first podcast.

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:17.770
It's called The Debate
Without Debate Podcast.

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:20.220
And our sole mission of that podcast

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:22.950
is to end polarization
through conversation,

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:25.230
just like we're having right now.

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:29.010
And so based off of that, our
main question to you today is

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:32.120
Gary, do you view yourself
as a polarizing figure

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:34.320
and if so, what are some of the benefits

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:35.893
that you've received from that?

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:37.750
- I don't.

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:41.060
I do understand that some people do.

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:44.750
I am comfortable speaking
my mind about things

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:46.240
I genuinely believe in.

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:48.553
I'm not trying to, you know...

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:54.340
I think anybody can be viewed polarizing

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:55.860
just on the fact that we live in a

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:58.520
politicized environment where one opinion

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:01.270
that you actually believe, in a nice way,

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:04.090
may not jive with somebody else's.

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:06.730
I tend to be more polarizing in the

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:07.913
businesses that I'm in.

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:11.670
I challenge status quo a lot,

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:13.740
which people view as polarizing.

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:15.423
I view myself as practical.

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:18.520
You know, I happen to communicate

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:20.757
in a Jersey style that I think...

00:02:21.885 --> 00:02:23.700
I think cursing may seem polarizing,

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:27.830
high energy and conviction
may seem polarizing,

00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:31.660
not accepting the norms of society may...

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:34.508
Actually, I'm clearly very polarizing.

00:02:34.508 --> 00:02:36.717
(laugh)

00:02:36.717 --> 00:02:41.170
Look, I think "polarizing,"
much like other words are...

00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:42.183
You know, "ego" and "confidence",

00:02:42.183 --> 00:02:44.160
people think they're the same,

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:46.190
I think they're completely different.

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:48.730
Polarizing and passionately practical,

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:51.280
I think are very similar
but wildly different

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:53.823
and I think I'm passionately practical.

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:56.910
- See, as an up-and-coming media star,

00:02:56.910 --> 00:02:58.510
we find it kind of difficult

00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:00.340
to cut through the noise sometimes.

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:02.470
It seems like most
traditional forms of media

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:04.080
or social media for that matter

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:08.093
kind of emphasizes this
polarizing, extreme nature.

00:03:08.093 --> 00:03:10.650
Gary, do you think there's even a market

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:12.854
for non-polarizing content, and if so,

00:03:12.854 --> 00:03:14.840
what does that business model look like

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:16.133
on an applicable scale?

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:18.890
- There's an ungodly amount of it.

00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:21.750
I mean, I haven't taken the time

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:24.860
to really audit the things
that you would decide

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:27.070
with your subjective
opinions aren't polarizing,

00:03:27.070 --> 00:03:30.120
but for every extreme Fox or extreme CNN,

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:33.127
or even me, I get that I'm high energy,

00:03:33.127 --> 00:03:37.280
I think entertainment works

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:39.370
and I think information works.

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:42.270
And I think entertainment
has taken over the news

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:45.720
in a lot of ways and that's
led to a lot of muckery.

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:49.590
But I'm sure there's
scientific journals that have

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:51.630
great B-to-B businesses
that do quite well.

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:52.710
There's an ungodly amount of

00:03:52.710 --> 00:03:56.250
business people I respect
that are not over-the-top.

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:57.690
Mary Meeker puts out one of the

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:02.690
best reports each year on
start-up and VC culture.

00:04:03.350 --> 00:04:06.740
She's not polarizing
by people's standards.

00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:09.780
Is Sheryl Sandberg's "Lean
In" a polarizing book?

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:11.873
For some, yes, for others, no.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:15.820
I think people look at
production and think

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:17.030
that's part of the polarization.

00:04:17.030 --> 00:04:20.170
What I mean by that is,
there's a lot of people

00:04:20.170 --> 00:04:22.870
articulating incredibly thoughtful work

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:26.460
on YouTube, podcasts, Medium, the press,

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:30.770
and there's just an ungodly
amount of good content

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:33.990
that comes across very calm.

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:35.540
Like, do you consider most of

00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:37.580
NPR's content polarizing?

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:40.830
If you're a Republican hardcore, you may

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:42.580
'cause you don't like
the things that are said.

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:44.960
But I think in the way it's delivered,

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:47.080
it's quite calm, you know.

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:50.370
And does extremely well,
I'm sure that if you went

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:53.970
through the top 200 podcasts
right now on iTunes,

00:04:53.970 --> 00:04:55.800
and clicked them all, you would agree

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:58.070
that half of them are bombastic.

00:04:58.070 --> 00:05:02.050
Howard Stern, Barstool,
and I think you'd find

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:05.120
half of them are just
kind of quite mundane.

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:07.050
And then the question becomes,

00:05:07.050 --> 00:05:10.413
are we judging polarization
on style or substance?

00:05:11.659 --> 00:05:15.610
You know, I think for me, one
of the things that works is

00:05:15.610 --> 00:05:17.180
I think I have a style.

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:20.310
Which by the way, hampers
me in a lot of environments.

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:21.390
There's an enormous amount of people

00:05:21.390 --> 00:05:22.670
who don't like me without

00:05:22.670 --> 00:05:25.220
even having a sense of my substance,

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:27.854
they just don't like Jersey talk.

00:05:27.854 --> 00:05:30.620
And that's okay, I actually respect that,

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:32.110
I'm empathetic to that.

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:36.720
But I think there's always
a market for good stuff,

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:39.840
and yes, does sizzle sell steaks?

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:42.380
Yup, you know what else sells steaks?

00:05:42.380 --> 00:05:44.780
Delicious fucking steaks
that don't have sizzle.

00:05:45.830 --> 00:05:46.670
- Love it, love it.

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:49.770
Gary, if we could have one last question.

00:05:49.770 --> 00:05:52.126
Given that you've
started your own podcast,

00:05:52.126 --> 00:05:54.640
GaryVee Audio Experience,
we love that stuff.

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:56.100
What would you do if you had

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:58.060
zero pre-existing social capital,

00:05:58.060 --> 00:06:00.723
you were Beta Gary, and you
wanted to just launch a podcast?

00:06:00.723 --> 00:06:02.810
We have a lot of listeners

00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:04.120
who are interested in starting again,

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:05.105
I know they--

00:06:05.105 --> 00:06:09.783
- I did it on February 21st, 2006.

00:06:09.783 --> 00:06:12.770
- It was called Wine Library
TV and nine people watched.

00:06:12.770 --> 00:06:15.663
- So what can you do to grow?

00:06:17.444 --> 00:06:20.120
- Aside from just producing content.

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:23.270
- So there's two things.

00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:25.070
The biggest thing that will help you grow

00:06:25.070 --> 00:06:27.710
is something that allows
you to grow sustainable,

00:06:27.710 --> 00:06:31.180
which is putting out quality content

00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:33.670
and quality content doesn't
mean the production quality,

00:06:33.670 --> 00:06:35.610
it means other people on the other side

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:38.270
find you funny, smart,
interesting, entertaining,

00:06:38.270 --> 00:06:39.773
period, end of story.

00:06:41.530 --> 00:06:44.130
There's always ways to be smart

00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:47.160
about your post-production
and your distribution.

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:49.800
It's called making the content contextual

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:51.710
to the 15 websites that I talk about

00:06:51.710 --> 00:06:53.780
fuckin' every second of my life.

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:55.000
LinkedIn and Tik--
- Yes, sir.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:56.640
- The reason I bring
up LinkedIn and TikTok

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:58.773
is because there's organic reach.

00:06:58.773 --> 00:07:01.100
But that doesn't mean that
if you don't have dollars

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:03.520
that Facebook and
Instagram are scary good.

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:06.300
The ability to do what I
did on Twitter still exists.

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:09.060
Go into Twitter, search the
words of your subject matter

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:11.470
and literally reply to
every human that's on there.

00:07:11.470 --> 00:07:13.400
Are you willing to do
it for 14 hours a day

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:17.080
like I did for four years
with limited results?

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:18.670
Most people don't have that conviction.

00:07:18.670 --> 00:07:21.260
Most people don't love
what they do enough.

00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:22.450
I don't want people to do things

00:07:22.450 --> 00:07:23.870
for 14 hours and burn out either.

00:07:23.870 --> 00:07:27.100
Put in three hours, but most
people put in zero hours.

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:30.100
Asher and Joey, most people
are putting in zero hours

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:32.290
of going into Twitter,
searching the conversation

00:07:32.290 --> 00:07:33.820
and replying to 40 people.

00:07:33.820 --> 00:07:34.910
Zero.

00:07:34.910 --> 00:07:35.867
- Very true.

00:07:35.867 --> 00:07:38.660
- People have audacity and expect

00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:40.290
that people are just gonna follow them.

00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:41.453
It's audacious.

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:43.833
- Very true.

00:07:43.833 --> 00:07:45.785
Gary, pleasure, really a pleasure.

00:07:45.785 --> 00:07:46.618
- Thank you, guys.

00:07:46.618 --> 00:07:47.451
- We look forward to
seeing you again soon.

00:07:47.451 --> 00:07:49.050
- Can't wait, hope so, take care guys.

00:07:50.890 --> 00:07:51.723
Audacity.

00:07:53.900 --> 00:07:54.750
Let's keep going.

00:07:56.590 --> 00:07:58.120
Tanner, what's good?

00:07:58.120 --> 00:07:59.353
- Hey Gary, how's it going?

00:07:59.353 --> 00:08:01.920
- It's going really well, and you?

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:02.870
- Good, good.

00:08:02.870 --> 00:08:04.230
- Good.

00:08:04.230 --> 00:08:05.830
- So a little bit of background.

00:08:06.700 --> 00:08:10.080
My dad started a karate school
when I was four years old,

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:14.703
and I grew up learning the
martial arts, started competing,

00:08:16.500 --> 00:08:20.743
and 2014 I won a world championship.

00:08:22.310 --> 00:08:25.140
So my whole life I've been doing this

00:08:25.140 --> 00:08:26.620
